{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-49014",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14208490/assets/81d16bb54bdecca628d769cd08d02dd327c5483851c2421fb595446c34f8a124.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the station and train $A,$\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the station and train $B,$\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the two trains.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $y,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=51\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=68\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {51}^{2}+{68}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =7\\,225 \\\\ z & =85\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=153\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=35\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(51)(153)+2(68)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(85)(35) \\\\ 136\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =5\\,950-15\\,606 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{9\\,656}{136} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-71.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of train $B$ is $71\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$",
